// `std/compiler.run_file` and the `std/compiler.RunFile` struct it takes — the
// row `15-app-and-defer/effects/effects_test.nomi` was sole-blocked on, whose
// whole item list was one site: `stdlib type | std/compiler.RunFile` at 4:30, a
// STRUCT-LITERAL CONSTRUCTION in an argument.
//
// # What is being crossed here, and it is two boundaries rather than one
//
// `run_file` differs from `run` in exactly two ways, and each is a boundary a
// wrong implementation would get wrong silently:
//
//  1. THE SOURCE COMES FROM DISK, resolved as a module path under the current
//     PROJECT ROOT. `target.nomi` beside this file is that source, and a
//     compiled artifact reaches the root through the boot shim's
//     `stdcompilerrun.SetProjectRoot` — so a row that finds the file proves the
//     shim ran, which no `compiler.run` fixture can show.
//  2. A SIMULATED PROCESS ENVIRONMENT rides along in `RunFile.env`, visible to
//     `Map.get(startup.env, ...)` inside the run source. `compiler.run` has no parameter for it and
//     passes nil.
//
// Everything else — the pipeline, the `Result<String, String>` marshalling, the
// discarding of partial output on a fault — is `compiler.run`'s, reached through
// the same eval.RunSource, and testdata/compiler_run.nomi already covers it. This
// fixture deliberately does not re-cover it.
//
// # THE `env` DEFAULT IS NOT OBSERVABLE HERE, AND THAT IS WORTH STATING
//
// `RunFile.env` is declared `Map<String, String> = Map.empty()`, so the builder
// must supply a value where a construction site omits the field. It does — see
// stdEmptyMapDefault — and NO OUTPUT IN THIS FIXTURE CAN TELL whether it did,
// because an omitted `rt.Map` field's Go zero value IS a perfectly good empty
// map: nil trie root, zero count. Rows 1 and 2 below are the same observation for
// two different reasons, deliberately, so that coincidence is recorded rather
// than mistaken for coverage.
//
// This is the failure mode the contract calls "a default whose value is the zero
// value collapses three derivations into one observation", and the honest answer
// is that the default is guarded somewhere else: `stdStructSpec.matches` requires
// std's declared default to have the shape `Map.empty()`, and
// TestStdStructSpecsMatchStdSource fails if it does not — at which point NO
// anchor is built and every mention of `RunFile` refuses loudly. So a std edit to
// that default is caught; a missing builder default is not, and could not be by
// any program.
//
// Contrast `Project.manifest`, whose default is `None`: a zero `Maybe` has Tag 0,
// which is neither Some nor None and matches no `case` arm, so THAT default is
// load-bearing against a wrong answer and its fixture can see it.
import {
    std/compiler.{self, RunFile}
    std/io
}

fn out(r: RunFile): String {
    case compiler.run_file(r) {
        Ok(text) -> "[" + text + "]"
        Err(message) -> "ERR " + message
    }
}

/// Whether the Err message mentions `needle`, for the one rejection whose text
/// is not pinnable.
///
/// A module that is simply ABSENT reports the OS's own error, which embeds the
/// ABSOLUTE path it tried — machine-dependent, so `out` would make this fixture
/// unpinnable on another checkout. The differential half does not care (both
/// paths produce the same path, which is itself the property that the compiled
/// artifact resolved the same project root), so what is dropped here is only the
/// absolute pin, and only for this row.
fn err_mentions(r: RunFile, needle: String): String {
    case compiler.run_file(r) {
        Ok(_) -> "unexpectedly ok"
        Err(message) -> "${String.contains?(message, needle)}"
    }
}

fn main() {
    // --- 1. THE FIELD DEFAULT PATH: `env` omitted entirely ------------------
    // The construction site the corpus writes. `probe=absent` shows the run
    // source saw no override for that key — which is also what an inherited real
    // process environment gives, since nothing sets it.
    io.print("default = " + out(RunFile{entry_point: "target"}))

    // --- 2. THE EXPLICIT-EMPTY PATH, same observation, different derivation --
    // Identical output to row 1 BY CONSTRUCTION. Present so the coincidence is in
    // the fixture rather than in a reader's head; see the header.
    io.print("explicit_empty = " + out(RunFile{entry_point: "target", env: Map.empty()}))

    // --- 3. THE ENV ACTUALLY ARRIVES ---------------------------------------
    // The only row that distinguishes "env is threaded" from "env is dropped".
    // `run_file` and `run` differ here and nowhere else in the signature, so a
    // `run`-shaped implementation of `run_file` passes rows 1 and 2 and fails
    // this one.
    io.print("with_env = " + out(RunFile{
            entry_point: "target",
            env: {"NOMI_RUN_FILE_FIXTURE_PROBE" => "arrived"},
        }))

    // --- 4. A SECOND KEY, so the map is a MAP and not one slot -------------
    // Two entries with only one of them read. A projection that carried the first
    // entry only, or that overwrote, answers row 3's text here too.
    io.print("two_keys = " + out(RunFile{
            entry_point: "target",
            env: {
                "NOMI_RUN_FILE_FIXTURE_OTHER" => "ignored",
                "NOMI_RUN_FILE_FIXTURE_PROBE" => "second",
            },
        }))

    // --- 5. THE ENTRY-POINT CONTAINMENT RULES, each its own Err ------------
    // Four rejections, and each is an `Err(message)` a Nomi program reads rather
    // than a fault — so the text is observable and both paths must produce it.
    // They are the whole reason eval.RunFileSource exists as one exported
    // function instead of the artifact composing the lookup itself: a second copy
    // of these four messages is a divergence waiting to be written.
    io.print("empty = " + out(RunFile{entry_point: ""}))
    io.print("suffixed = " + out(RunFile{entry_point: "target.nomi"}))
    io.print("escaping = " + out(RunFile{entry_point: "../target"}))

    // The fourth rejection is the OS's own, and its text embeds the ABSOLUTE path
    // that was tried — so it is read for its shape rather than pinned. See
    // err_mentions.
    io.print(
        "missing = " + err_mentions(RunFile{entry_point: "no_such_module"}, "no_such_module.nomi")
    )
}
